<td id="S5.T1.4.14.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T1.4.14.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">100%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.14.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T1.4.14.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">100%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.14.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T1.4.14.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">100%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.14.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T1.4.14.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">88%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.14.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T1.4.14.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">100%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.14.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T1.4.14.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">100%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.14.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T1.4.14.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">99%</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.4.15" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.4.15.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.4.15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Improv.</span></th>
<td id="S5.T1.4.15.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.4.15.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">29.9%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.15.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.4.15.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">20.5%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.15.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.4.15.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">40.8%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.15.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.4.15.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">33.3%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.15.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.4.15.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">35.1%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.15.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.4.15.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">38.9%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.15.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.4.15.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">47.7%</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare TrojFill against five representative black-box jailbreak methods:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jailbroken</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>)</cite>: A suite of jailbreak techniques (e.g., Base64/encoding attacks, refusal suppression) that exploit competing objectives and generalization mismatches in LLMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PAIR</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: A pipeline that constructs system prompts and uses an attacker LLM to iteratively generate and refine adversarial prompts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPTFuzzer</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>: Starts from manually authored seed prompts and applies diverse mutation strategies (each driven by a dedicated mutation prompt) to rewrite and diversify candidates.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Past-tense attack</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andriushchenko and Flammarion, 2024</a>)</cite>: Uses an attacker LLM to reformulate unsafe instructions in the past tense to circumvent safety filters.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AutoDAN-Turbo</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>: A retrieval-augmented framework that queries a strategy library using the target LLM’s responses, and iteratively rewrites prompts with an attacker LLM; the attacker also summarizes rounds to update the strategy library.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Implementations and Settings</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate TrojFill on a range of commercial and open models spanning hundreds of billions to trillions of parameters: <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">2</span></span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://openai.com/index/hello-gpt-4o/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_medium">https://openai.com/index/hello-gpt-4o/</a></span></span></span></span> (<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>200B to 1.8T), <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemini-2.5-flash</span> and <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemini-2.5-pro</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Comanici et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-3.1<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">3</span></span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://huggingface.co/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V3.1" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_medium">https://huggingface.co/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V3.1</a></span></span></span></span> (671B), and <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen3-Max</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2025</a>)</cite> (&gt;1T). We also include two smaller models: <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4.1-mini<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">4</span></span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://platform.openai.com/docs/models/gpt-4.1-mini" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_medium">https://platform.openai.com/docs/models/gpt-4.1-mini</a></span></span></span></span> and <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Llama3-8B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI@Meta, 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For our method TrojFill, we use Gemini-2.5-flash as the attacker LLM for unsafe-term extraction and replacement (Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. Unsafe Terms Extraction and Replacement ‣ 4. Methods ‣ The Trojan Example: Jailbreaking LLMs through Template Filling and Unsafety Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>). We observe no substantial performance differences when using other attacker LLMs; all tested attacker models reliably perform the extraction/replacement task. The maximum number of iterations <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 10.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Additional implementation details and settings for the target LLMs (Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3. Target LLM Settings ‣ Appendix A Implementation Details ‣ The Trojan Example: Jailbreaking LLMs through Template Filling and Unsafety Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>), baseline configurations (Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4. Method-specific Settings ‣ Appendix A Implementation Details ‣ The Trojan Example: Jailbreaking LLMs through Template Filling and Unsafety Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>), evaluation criteria (Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1. Judgement ‣ Appendix A Implementation Details ‣ The Trojan Example: Jailbreaking LLMs through Template Filling and Unsafety Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>), and hardware setup (Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5. Hardware and execution ‣ Appendix A Implementation Details ‣ The Trojan Example: Jailbreaking LLMs through Template Filling and Unsafety Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>) are provided in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4. </span>Overall Performances</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1.2. Metrics ‣ 5.1. Evaluations ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ The Trojan Example: Jailbreaking LLMs through Template Filling and Unsafety Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports attack success rates (ASR) for TrojFill and baselines. Several consistent patterns emerge.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">By target model.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">TrojFill attains the highest LLM-judge ASR on every evaluated model (e.g., 97% on GPT-4o, 94% on GPT-4.1-mini, 100% on Gemini-2.5-flash and DeepSeek-3.1, 95% on Qwen-Max, 88% on Gemini-2.5-pro and 77% on Llama3-8B). Under the rule-based judge (which flags direct refusals), TrojFill reaches near-100% on most models and 99% on Llama3-8B. The Llama3-8B discrepancy (99% rule-judge vs. 77% LLM-judge) indicates that TrojFill successfully avoids explicit refusals on that model, but the model’s limited generation capacity sometimes prevents it from producing fully detailed jailbreak outputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">By method.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Simple obfuscation approaches (the <span id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jailbroken</span> suite) perform poorly under the LLM-judge, showing that basic encodings (e.g., Base64) no longer reliably fool modern models. Notably, Jailbroken achieves a nontrivial rule-judge ASR on Llama3-8B (67%) because that model occasionally treats obfuscated prompts as innocuous rather than explicitly refusing them. The <span id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Past-tense</span> attack attains relatively high rule-judge ASR but, upon inspection and under the LLM-judge, typically produces conceptual or historical descriptions rather than actionable jailbreak content. LLM-optimization methods (<span id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPTFuzzer</span>, <span id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PAIR</span>, <span id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AutoDAN-Turbo</span>) outperform simple perturbations but remain inferior to TrojFill; these methods largely preserve the original task formulation (directly requesting harmful content) and therefore fail to exploit the template-filling + unsafe-reasoning framing that drives TrojFill’s effectiveness.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21190v1/barchart.svg" id="S5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:469/188;" width="469" height="188"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Attack success rate on unsafe categories</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Judge distinction.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rule-based judge captures explicit refusals and canned rejection phrases, while the LLM-based judge evaluates semantic relevance and whether the response genuinely satisfies the unsafe objective. TrojFill’s strong results on both judges indicate it does more than avoid rejection phrasing: it elicits detailed, relevant outputs that a human or LLM judge considers successful jailbreaks. By contrast, several baselines either (i) avoid explicit rejection but produce irrelevant or non-actionable output (inflating rule-judge scores) or (ii) are rejected outright (deflating both metrics).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Category-level performance.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ By method. ‣ 5.4. Overall Performances ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ The Trojan Example: Jailbreaking LLMs through Template Filling and Unsafety Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows category-specific ASRs. Most models have very high ASR on categories such as <em id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Harassment</em>, <em id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Malware/Hacking</em>, and <em id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Physical harm</em>, but lower ASR on <em id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Disinformation</em> (e.g., fabricating that the Earth is flat). This likely reflects that factual knowledge is strongly reinforced during pre-training and anti-hallucination efforts, making deliberate fabrication harder even when other safeguards are bypassed. The gap is model-dependent: stronger models (GPT-4o, Gemini-2.5-flash, DeepSeek-3.1) maintain high ASR across categories, while smaller models (e.g., Llama3-8B) struggle on categories requiring precise factual inversion or nuanced reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, TrojFill significantly outperforms existing black-box baselines across models and evaluation metrics, demonstrating both robustness and transferability. Its model-specific limitations are mainly due to generation capacity and factual grounding. A <span id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">case study</span> in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Case Study ‣ The Trojan Example: Jailbreaking LLMs through Template Filling and Unsafety Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> illustrates that TrojFill can effectively jailbreak diverse categories and handle various text types.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5. </span>Parameter study</h3>

<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2. </span>Ablation Study of Our Method</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T2.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Metic</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Method <math id="S5.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math> / LLM <math id="S5.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.4.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GPT-4o</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.4.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GPT-4.1-mini</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.4.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Gemini-2.5-flash</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.4.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Gemini-2.5-pro</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.4.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">DeepSeek-3.1</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.4.1.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qwen-Max</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.4.1.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Llama3-8B</span></th></tr>
</thead>
