<td id="S5.T2.4.12.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T2.4.12.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">97%</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.4.13" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.4.13.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><span id="S5.T2.4.13.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TrojFill</span></th>
<td id="S5.T2.4.13.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.4.13.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">100%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.13.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.4.13.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">100%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.13.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.4.13.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">100%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.13.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.4.13.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">88%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.13.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.4.13.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">100%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.13.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.4.13.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">100%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.13.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.4.13.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">99%</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21190v1/lines_llm.svg" id="S5.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/179;" width="238" height="179"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>ASR (LLM Evaluation)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21190v1/lines_rule.svg" id="S5.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/179;" width="238" height="179"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>ASR (Rule-Based Evaluation)</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Attack Success Rates Across Different Iteration Counts (<math id="S5.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>)</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the effect of the number of optimization iterations <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.5. Parameter study ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ The Trojan Example: Jailbreaking LLMs through Template Filling and Unsafety Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> reports Attack Success Rates (ASR) as <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> varies. Two clear patterns emerge: (1) most models reach their peak ASR within 10 iterations, and (2) even a single iteration already yields substantial success, e.g., Gemini-2.5-flash attains <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>92% ASR at iteration 1 and most LLMs exceed 70% ASR on the first try. These results show fast convergence and diminishing returns after a few iterations, motivating a small practical choice of <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (we use <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=5</annotation></semantics></math> in most experiments).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We further provide a cost analysis to quantify the practical expenses of our method. Specifically, we consider <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o</span> as the target model ($2.50 per 1M input tokens and $10 per 1M output tokens, <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the most expensive LLM evaluated in our experiments</span>) and <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemini-2.5-flash</span> as the attacker LLM ($0.30 per 1M input tokens and $2.50 per 1M output tokens). Based on typical token usage observed in our pipeline, the estimated per-sample costs are approximately:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One-time preprocessing (unsafe-term extraction + text-type recognition): <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx\$0.00014" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">≈</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">$</mo><mn>0.00014</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx\$0.00014</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Per iteration: attacker rewriting <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx\$0.00010" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">≈</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">$</mo><mn>0.00010</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx\$0.00010</annotation></semantics></math>; target-LLM interaction <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx\$0.00358" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">≈</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">$</mo><mn>0.00358</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx\$0.00358</annotation></semantics></math>; total per iteration <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx\$0.00368" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">≈</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">$</mo><mn>0.00368</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx\$0.00368</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=5</annotation></semantics></math> (empirically sufficient for GPT-4o), the total cost per jailbreak attempt is therefore roughly</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.00014+5\times 0.00368\approx\$0.0185," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0.00014</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>0.00368</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≈</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">$</mo><mn>0.0185</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.00014+5\times 0.00368\approx\$0.0185,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">an economical budget compared with prior iterative black-box attacks. These numbers assume the token counts and provider prices above; the qualitative conclusion, fast convergence and low per-sample cost, holds across reasonable variations in token usage and pricing.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.6. </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate five TrojFill variants to assess the contribution of individual components:</p>
<ul id="S5.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transformation:</span> Only transformed instructions without the template.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TrojFill<math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">raw</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> Template with raw unsafe instructions, without transformation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TrojFill<math id="S5.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{w/o pre}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">w/o pre</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{w/o pre}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> Template without the prefix paragraph.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TrojFill<math id="S5.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{w/o suf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">w/o suf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{w/o suf}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> Template without the suffix requesting unsafety reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TrojFill<math id="S5.I3.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{w/o text\_type}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">w/o text_type</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{w/o text\_type}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> Replaces <span id="S5.I3.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[text type]</span> with a generic "text".</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.5. Parameter study ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ The Trojan Example: Jailbreaking LLMs through Template Filling and Unsafety Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> compares these variants across multiple LLMs. We observe that <span id="S5.SS6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transformation-only</span> achieves very low attack success rates (ASR) on stronger models (e.g., 5% on GPT-4o), showing that transformation alone cannot reliably bypass safety. In contrast, <span id="S5.SS6.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TrojFill<math id="S5.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">raw</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which leverages the template structure without transformation, already achieves notable success on advanced models (e.g., 88% on Gemini-2.5-flash), highlighting the effectiveness of the template in inducing unsafe outputs. Interestingly, smaller models such as Llama3-8B show the opposite trend: transformation significantly boosts ASR (58%) compared to template-only prompts (5%), reflecting their stricter refusal behavior when explicit unsafe terms appear <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI@Meta, 2024</a>)</cite>.
Variants without the prefix or suffix confirm that both parts contribute to jailbreak effectiveness: dropping the prefix has mild impact, while removing the suffix (i.e., unsafety reasoning) reduces ASR more substantially. Finally, removing <span id="S5.SS6.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[text type]</span> slightly lowers performance, as outputs may deviate from the intended format, though refusal rates remain largely unaffected. Overall, the complete <span id="S5.SS6.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TrojFill</span> consistently yields the highest ASR across all models, demonstrating that each component plays a complementary role in jailbreak effectiveness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.7. </span>Transferability</h3>

<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.21190v1/heatmap_LLM.png" id="S5.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:238/204;" width="238" height="204" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>ASR (LLM Evaluation)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.21190v1/heatmap_Rule.png" id="S5.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:238/204;" width="238" height="204" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>ASR (Rule-Based Evaluation)</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>Transferability of Jailbreaking Prompts Across Target LLMs</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present the transferability results in Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.7. Transferability ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ The Trojan Example: Jailbreaking LLMs through Template Filling and Unsafety Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Each cell in the heatmaps represents the ratio of successful prompts on the x-axis LLM that were originally optimized on the y-axis LLM. For instance, the cell at coordinates <math id="S5.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,5)</annotation></semantics></math> in Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.7. Transferability ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ The Trojan Example: Jailbreaking LLMs through Template Filling and Unsafety Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(a), with a value of 91, indicates that prompts generated when GPT-4.1-mini served as the target model achieve a 91% ASR on GPT-4o. Since each entry measures one-way transfer performance, the matrix is not symmetric along the diagonal. The results reveal strong cross-model transferability: prompts optimized on one advanced LLM (e.g., GPT-4o, Gemini-2.5-flash) tend to generalize well to others, suggesting that TrojFill produces robust and reusable jailbreak prompts. Interestingly, however, prompts generated from other models transfer poorly to Llama3-8B. This can be attributed to Llama3’s weaker reasoning and decoding capabilities: it often fails to correctly interpret Caesar or Base64-encoded instructions, leading to irrelevant outputs. Conversely, the successful prompts obtained from Llama3-8B are typically based on the “text-splitting” transformation, which generalizes relatively well to other LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Limitations and Discussions</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While TrojFill performs well on many commercial LLMs (e.g., ChatGPT, Gemini, DeepSeek and Qwen) as the experimental results show, it does not work on Anthropic’s recent Claude models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2024</a>)</cite>. In our experiments all evaluated black-box baselines also fail on Claude; we attribute this to Claude’s external <em id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">constitutional</em> input/output classifiers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which filter both prompts and outputs and therefore block the relevant generations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We note that some earlier studies reported strong jailbreak performance on older Claude models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andriushchenko et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; however, those results predate the integration of constitutional classifiers and fail to generalize to newer versions. While such external classifiers are effective, they come with notable drawbacks: (1) they increase computational overhead (e.g., adding 23.7% overhead in Claude’s implementation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2025</a>)</cite>), and (2) they are impractical for open-source LLMs, where users can easily disable these overlays. Consequently, most mainstream LLM providers either avoid relying on these classifiers or design them to be optional (as in Gemini).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, TrojFill, and most existing jailbreak methods, primarily probe internal, parametric safeguards (Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1. Safety Alignment ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ The Trojan Example: Jailbreaking LLMs through Template Filling and Unsafety Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). Developing techniques that reliably evade both internal defenses and external input/output classifiers is an important but technically and ethically sensitive direction for future red-teaming and alignment research.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduced <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TrojFill</span>, a black-box jailbreaking framework that reframes unsafe instruction as a <span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">template-filling task with unsafety reasoning</span>.
Our approach embeds transformed unsafe instructions into a structured prompt that first requests a detailed example and then performs sentence-by-sentence analysis.
The key "example" component effectively serves as a Trojan Horse, embedding the target jailbreak content within a context that minimizes the likelihood of model refusal.
Through extensive evaluations, TrojFill demonstrates strong attack performance on leading commercial LLMs (e.g., ChatGPT and Gemini) and exhibits high transferability across different models.
We hope this work contributes to a deeper understanding of LLM vulnerabilities and aids future research in safety alignment and robust red-teaming.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="bib" class="ltx_bibliography">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_bibliography">References</h2>

<ul class="ltx_biblist">
      
<li id="bib.bib2" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">AI@Meta (2024)</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">
AI@Meta. 2024.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">Llama 3 Model Card.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">(2024).

